NOW native endpoint prototype (V3, logical retirement)
=====================================================

Scope and compatibility
------------------------

This revision removes the experimental retained-channel lease and worker
dispatcher. It adds no shared worker messages, shutdown/admission subsystem,
channel refcounts, table retention, public drdynvc ABI, global synchronous-DVC
setting, or generic_dynvc behavior changes. NOW uses its own listener.

NowProtoClientContext remains exactly { handle, custom, Write, Read }. Existing
NativeSessions consumers receive it through the same listener-level connected/
disconnected events. Legacy mode rejects a second simultaneous offer, uses
Read/Write, and keeps the context session-owned. Legacy Write now validates
nonempty bytes and uses the same protected write path; zero-length Write is
deliberately rejected rather than using drdynvc's implicit Close behavior.
Callers must stop using the legacy context before native teardown.

Direct V3 opt-in accepts overlapping offers and provides retained per-offer
identities. It disables legacy dispatch (legacy Write returns ERROR_INVALID_STATE).
No NativeSessions/RDM production source or package is changed here.

Public ABI and registration
---------------------------

<freerdp/client/nowproto.h> declares:

  UINT freerdp_nowproto_client_set_callbacks(
      NowProtoClientContext*, const NowProtoClientCallbacksV3*);

  NowProtoClientCallbacksV3:
    UINT32 size;       // sizeof(NowProtoClientCallbacksV3)
    UINT32 version;    // NOWPROTO_CLIENT_CALLBACKS_VERSION_3 (3)
    void* custom;
    void Offer(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint);
    void Receive(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint,
                 const BYTE* data, size_t size);
    void Closed(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint);

  NowProtoEndpoint (ordered function pointers):
    void Retain(NowProtoEndpoint*);
    void Release(NowProtoEndpoint*);
    UINT Write(NowProtoEndpoint*, const BYTE* data, UINT32 size);
    UINT Retire(NowProtoEndpoint*);

Native C pointer alignment/size_t and calling conventions apply. This is V3,
not either published experimental V2 contract; old version/layout is rejected.
All callbacks are required; size/version are validated. The table is copied,
installed once, and frozen. Install before first OnOpen: the first NOW
OnChannelConnected is early enough. Managed settings can choose opt-in before
Connect, then attach when the native context becomes available.

The null-context registration probe returns ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER (87)
without dereferencing the table or causing side effects. An old binary without
the export must be reported as unsupported, not silently use the legacy proxy.
Native registration success is CHANNEL_RC_OK (0). The DevolutionsRdp managed
listener wrapper has a DIFFERENT convention: nonzero means handled; its
void PubSub subscriber does not propagate managed failure into native Connect.
Bindings must explicitly surface registration failure, including when a channel
arrives after Connect has returned.

Offer, write, retirement and native closure
-------------------------------------------

Each Offer has a distinct endpoint, never a reusable channel ID. Offer occurs on
OnOpen after CREATE response. Receive and Closed always carry that identity.
No native code selects a winning replacement or automatically retires its
predecessor. The listener-level pInterface remains the legacy context.

Callbacks borrow the endpoint and receive buffer. Retain the endpoint before
handing it to asynchronous work; copy receive bytes. Release every owned
reference, even after retirement/native teardown. Each operation requires an
owned reference; do not race the final Release with a new operation.
Retained tombstones do not retain or dereference the freed plugin/session.

Write takes nonnull/nonempty bytes, copies/submits synchronously through the
existing native transport, and returns its result. The caller must keep the
buffer alive until return. No native command queue, token or completion callback
exists. Null/empty input returns ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER; retirement/native
closure returns ERROR_NOT_CONNECTED. Write errors do not synthesize Closed.
The result is NOT remote acknowledgement or guaranteed wire delivery. Base
drdynvc still maps some disconnected-send results to success. An admitted write
can race remote closure and be discarded by the peer.

Retire is idempotent LOCAL retirement. It closes future write/receive admission
and waits out a write already using the endpoint. It never calls native Close,
sends CLOSE, or synthesizes Closed. A Receive admitted before concurrent Retire
may run or finish after Retire returns. Managed actors must fence queued events
by their own generation/endpoint ownership; callbacks do not publish a winner.

Closed means actual native closure, once per offered endpoint, on remote close
or normal session teardown. It still occurs after Retire. A retired native
channel consumes resources until the peer/session closes it; this prototype
cannot force a stalled peer to close or reuse that wire ID. Applications can
accept a replacement with a different wire ID in the meantime.

Default asynchronous DVC mode stays unchanged. Callbacks run on the native DVC
thread (drdynvc worker by default). They only copy/enqueue nonblocking work:
no inline active Write, managed waits, recursive native pumping or session
destruction. Retain/Release and Retire are safe from callbacks, but Retire can
wait for a native write, so actor-side retirement is preferred. No NOW lock is
held during user callbacks; core receive/close can hold the channel-table lock.
Root delegates, callback code and custom state until native teardown returns,
NOT until logical disposal. Closed can release a consumer endpoint reference.

The lock/lifetime argument and sole core correction
---------------------------------------------------

An endpoint-local lock E covers raw Write from pointer validation through return.
OnClose is called BEFORE native free, and must acquire E to tombstone transport.
An atomic retired bit closes admission before waiting for E, so producers cannot
keep admitting new writes during retirement/OnClose. Receive only checks that
bit; it does not wait behind native writes or call user code with E held.

On this base, nonempty raw Write takes the channel lock C but no channel-table
lock T; its error paths do not call OnClose. Empty Write can synchronously close,
so both legacy/direct paths reject it. No path invokes raw Close. Endpoint
operations touch no plugin or table after obtaining the retained endpoint.

One localized shared fix remains: dvcchannel_send_close takes existing C around
drdynvc_send, then releases it BEFORE OnClose. Without this, core close send
updates the SAME stats.bytesOut as a concurrent nonempty Write before reaching
NOW's OnClose gate. The fix also keeps CLOSE out of the middle of fragmented
Write submission. It changes no callback, ownership, interface or lifecycle.
The entire diff in drdynvc_main.c relative to d0584b3a is +5/-1 lines.

Lock order is writer E -> C; remote close T -> C (released) -> E.
No C is held while waiting for E, and writers never need T: no cycle.
Normal drdynvc disconnect joins its worker after closing channels, before
destroying plugin/manager/transport. OnClose therefore waits out retained
writers before free. Caller-owned session lifecycle must still follow normal
Disconnect then Terminated; arbitrary concurrent destruction/late transport
callbacks, inline session destruction, module unload while endpoints retain
function pointers, and broken OS synchronization are not supported.

Native callbacks can briefly wait for an in-flight native submission; producers
should bound message sizes/outstanding work. This is not a hard-real-time or
lock-free API. No native negotiation, timeouts, admission limits or actor policy.

Validation
-----------

Use BUILD_TESTING=OFF, BUILD_TESTING_INTERNAL=ON, WITH_CLIENT_COMMON=ON and
CHANNEL_NOWPROTO_CLIENT=ON. Targets: TestNowProto and TestDrdynvc.

  cmake --build build/now-native --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  ctest --test-dir build/now-native \
    -R 'Test(NowProto(Lifetime|Faults)|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
    --repeat until-fail:50 --output-on-failure --timeout 60

Passed all three tests, 50 repetitions each on macOS arm64. The async cases use
the DEFAULT setting and real worker/PDU dispatch, with a test-only barrier
listener, not a production dispatcher. Coverage includes legacy ABI, overlap,
stale/reused IDs, retirement without native CLOSE/Closed, receive suppression,
already-admitted callbacks, reentrant retirement/reference release, transport
errors/pre-OnOpen discard, forced fragmented-write/remote-close ordering,
retire/write and disconnect/write overlap, repeated concurrent producers versus
remote close, retained tombstones after teardown/reconnect, and a separate
synchronous-mode compatibility case. NOW-local fault shims cover allocation,
critical-section init, callback-table validation/copying and failure cleanup.
No production fault hooks are added.

  cmake --build build/now-native-asan --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
    ctest --test-dir build/now-native-asan \
      -R 'Test(NowProto(Lifetime|Faults)|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
      --repeat until-fail:20 --output-on-failure --timeout 60

Passed all three tests, 20 repetitions each. LeakSanitizer is disabled on macOS;
WITH_UNICODE_BUILTIN=OFF avoids previously reproduced unrelated Unicode UB.
Build/validation logs use narrow-build.log and narrow-validation.log.

  cmake --build build/now-native-tsan --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
    ctest --test-dir build/now-native-tsan \
      -R 'Test(NowProto(Lifetime|Faults)|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
      --output-on-failure --timeout 60

TSan is NOT clean: existing timer-thread and WinPR critical.c lock-metadata
races block full race-detector coverage. No suppressions/unrelated fixes.

A build-only, ignored bridge/fixture in build/now-bundle-probe verifies that
dlopen(DevolutionsRdp) resolves the real registration export from its dependency.
NativeSessions also exercised actual managed V3 table/endpoint marshaling,
registration, receives, retirement, native closure/reopen and teardown against
that real plugin fixture. The fixture uses a fake transport/calling thread;
it complements rather than replaces the native async-worker tests. It is not
a shipping Conan/package validation.

Remaining Windows parity limits are deliberate: no active native Close or
prompt peer cleanup, no delivery acknowledgement, and actor-owned async policy
is outside native code. These native tests and ABI smoke are not live-server,
Linux runtime or Windows/RDM supervision parity claims.
